      "rev_n_1_0914df_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_0914df_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_0914df",
        "thought_text": "Model the problem as a graph where nodes represent cities and edges represent flights. Use a modified Dijkstra's algorithm to find the cheapest path for each jury member to travel to Metropolis and then back home, considering the k-day constraint.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412401.099288
      },
      "rev_n_2_3230c6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_3230c6_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_3230c6",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming to keep track of the minimum cost to bring each jury member to Metropolis for k days and then return home. Iterate over days and cities to update the costs based on available flights.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412401.099309
      },
      "rev_n_3_79eefb_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_79eefb_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_79eefb",
        "thought_text": "Sort flights by departure day and use a greedy approach to select the cheapest flights that allow all jury members to be in Metropolis for k days and then return home, ensuring that no conflicts arise in flight schedules.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412401.099322
      },
      "rev_n_4_2414a0_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_2414a0_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_2414a0",
        "thought_text": "The current approach might be too simplistic by only considering the earliest available flights without accounting for the possibility of overlapping flights or delays that could affect the overall cost. A more sophisticated scheduling algorithm that considers multiple scenarios and constraints might be necessary.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4540046709487321133",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412489.505406
      },
      "rev_n_5_5801df_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_5801df_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_5801df",
        "thought_text": "Another potential issue could be the handling of the k-day requirement. The current implementation does not explicitly ensure that each jury member spends exactly k days in Metropolis, which could lead to incorrect results. Implementing a check to verify this condition after processing all flights might help.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4540046709487321133",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412489.50544
      },
      "rev_n_6_2919d3_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_2919d3_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_2919d3",
        "thought_text": "Considering the large input sizes (up to 10^5 cities and flights), the current solution might be inefficient. Optimizing the data structures used for storing and accessing flight information, such as using binary search trees or hash maps with better average-case performance, could improve the execution time and reduce the likelihood of timeouts.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4540046709487321133",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412489.505455
      },
      "rev_n_7_f7f82f_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_f7f82f_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_f7f82f",
        "thought_text": "Revisit the Dijkstra's algorithm implementation to ensure it correctly handles the constraints and updates the state for each city. Specifically, check how the `visited` array is being updated and accessed to avoid index errors.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7396959648969254120",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412537.7750552
      },
      "rev_n_8_db1cf2_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_db1cf2_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_db1cf2",
        "thought_text": "Consider using a different approach, such as dynamic programming, to keep track of the minimum cost to bring each jury member to Metropolis and back, while respecting the k-day constraint. This might involve creating a 2D DP table where dp[i][j] represents the minimum cost to bring the first i jury members to Metropolis for j days.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7396959648969254120",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412537.7750778
      },
      "rev_n_9_f5c2a4_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_f5c2a4_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_f5c2a4",
        "thought_text": "Implement a modified version of the Floyd-Warshall algorithm to find the shortest paths between all pairs of cities for each day. This could help in identifying the optimal sequence of flights for each jury member to minimize the total cost while ensuring they spend at least k days in Metropolis.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7396959648969254120",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794412537.7750888
      }
